<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-6-7-exercises" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <p>
    Please complete the following problems. Show your work and simplify your answers whenever possible.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-1-ssn">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The social security number  of a person is a sequence of nine digits that are not necessarily distinct. If <m>X</m> is the set of all social security numbers, find the number of elements of <m>X</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-2-cards">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the number of ways of picking:
        <ol>
          <li><p>a king and a queen;</p></li>
          <li><p>a king or a queen;</p></li>
          <li><p>a king and a red card;</p></li>
          <li><p>a king or a queen;</p></li>
        </ol>
        from a deck of cards.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-3-letters">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the number of ways to form a four-letter sequence using the letters A, B, C, D, and E, if:
        <ol>
          <li><p>repetitions of letters are permitted.</p></li>
          <li><p>repetitions of letters are not permitted.</p></li>
          <li><p>the sequence contains the letter A but repetitions of letters are permitted.</p></li>
          <li><p>the sequence contains the letter A but repetitions of letters are not permitted.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-4-bridges">
    <statement>
      <p>
        There are three bridges connecting two towns, A and B. Between towns B and C, there are four bridges. A traveler wishes to travel from town A to town C. Find:
        <ol>
          <li><p>the number of possible choices of bridges from A to C.</p></li>
          <li><p>the number of possible choices of bridges for a round-trip travel from A to C.</p></li>
          <li><p>the number of possible choices of bridges for a round-trip travel from A to C if no bridge is to be repeated.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-5-pascal">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove Pascal's formula,
        <md>
          \binom{n}{r} = \binom{n - 1}{r} + \binom{n - 1}{r - 1}
        </md>
        algebraically by using the definition of <m>\binom{n}{r}</m> in terms of factorials.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-6-byte">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <em>bit</em> is a binary digit, i.e., either a 0 or a 1. A <em>byte</em> is a sequence of eight bits. Find the number of bytes that can be formed using exactly six zeros.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-7-die">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose we roll a die and write down the number that shows us. Suppose we repeat this process until we've recorded five numbers, and then we add the results. The five throws constitute a trial. Find the number of possible trials for which the sum is at most 12.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ch-6-8-curriculum">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose athe Data Science department at some university offers 8 lower division courses, <m>\{L_1, L_2, \ldots, L_8\}</m> and 10 upper division courses, <m>\{U_1, U_2, \ldots, U_{10}\}</m>. A <em>valid curriculum</em> consists of 4 lower division courses and 3 upper division courses.
        <ol>
          <li><p>How many different curricula are possible?</p></li>
          <li><p>Suppose that <m>\{U_1, \ldots, U_5\}</m> have <m>L_1</m> as a prerequisite, and <m>\{U_6, \ldots, U_{10}\}</m> have both <m>L_2</m> and <m>L_3</m> as a prerequisite. How many different curricula are possible?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</section>